<div class="wrap">
  <section class="home-hero">
    <h1>Systems programming,<br>down to the bedrock.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      <strong>Bedrock</strong> is an imperative, statically typed systems programming language
      built around simplicity, readability, and practicality, to provide the control of a systems
      language without the unecessary ceremony.
    </p>
    <p class="lede">
      Memory is managed automatically through a <strong>region-based memory model</strong>.
      No <code>new</code>, no <code>free</code>, no garbage collector. Explicit low-level
      control stays available when you need it, behind <code>unsafe</code> blocks.
    </p>
    <div class="home-cta">
      <a class="btn solid" href="/docs.html">Read the docs</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/grammar.html">View the grammar</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="home-cols">
    <div>
      <h3>Familiar by design</h3>
      <p>An imperative core: procedures, functions, structs, and modules. No classes,
      no inheritance, no hidden control flow.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Regions, not GC</h3>
      <p>Scoped arenas are bulk-freed the instant their block exits. Lifetimes are checked
      at compile time by a simple escape rule.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Explicit failure</h3>
      <p>Errors and optional values live in the type system: <code>File!</code> can fail,
      <code>?User</code> may be absent. Nothing fails silently.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>
